<p class="meta"><strong>May 2026 — first public formulation of the methodology</strong></p>
<p>The <strong>Punk Rock Orchestra (PRO)</strong> emerged during the investigation set in motion by Melissa 1.0. The problem had changed: beyond observing Human–AI Interaction phenomena, Nicchio needed a way to subject his own hypotheses to systematic criticism while working as an independent researcher, without a laboratory, a fixed team, or continuous access to human peers. The case motivated the method, but the paper does not offer it as evidence for the methodology.</p>
<p>The first version formalized a <strong>single-operator methodology for adversarial epistemic triangulation</strong>, in which different language-model instances are assigned distinct analytical functions and deliberately placed in tension. Rather than treating agreement among agents as evidence of correctness, PRO seeks resistance, criticism, and traceability before a conclusion is treated as plausible.</p>
<p>Concepts introduced in this first formulation included the <strong>N1 / N2 / N3</strong> specialization taxonomy, the distinction between <strong>Robotic Class</strong> and <strong>Dialogical Class</strong>, the <strong>Blue Team, Red Team, and Forensic Layer</strong> architecture, the <strong>Sterling Protocol</strong>, as well as the identification of <strong>context poisoning by provisioning failure</strong> and the architectural response termed the <strong>Cognitive Jelly Principle</strong>. The paper also frames PRO as a possible design for a deliberative layer complementary to execution-oriented systems — an architectural proposition rather than an empirical comparison already demonstrated.</p>
<figure class="thread-media pro-v1-architecture"><a href="/assets/media/thread/pro_v1_diagrama01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/assets/media/thread/pro_v1_diagrama01.jpg" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Diagram of the Punk Rock Orchestra v1 architecture with Blue Team, Red Team, and Forensic Layer" width="921" height="399"></a><figcaption><strong>PRO v1 architecture</strong><span class="thread-media-note">Blue Team, Red Team, and Forensic Layer. Crop of Figure 1 from the paper published in May 2026.</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The methodology was subjected to an initial Pilot Study with <strong>54 documented interactions</strong>, combining three specialization levels, three platforms — Claude, Gemini, and DeepSeek — and a common sequence of six adversarial prompts. The study found consistent N3 differentiation under pressure, while lower levels produced different outcomes depending on the platform.</p>
<p>The paper carefully limits that conclusion: the pilot measures <strong>epistemic integrity and stability under adversarial conditions</strong>; it does not demonstrate that N3 produces cognitively superior analysis on open-ended, non-adversarial problems. That construct-validity boundary opened the next phase of the research.</p>
<p><strong>PRO v1</strong> made the first complete architecture of the method public. The concepts introduced there would later be revisited, tested, and developed in greater depth in a new phase, leading to <strong>Punk Rock Orchestra v2</strong>.</p>
<p>The work has public records and instances across different research, preservation, and access ecosystems.</p><div class="evidence-list">
